---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import { CHROME_EXTENSION_URL } from "../../lib/site.js";

/**
 * Exeora for Chrome, from the point of view of someone deciding whether to
 * install it: what it shows, how it signs in, what it can reach, and how to
 * take it away again. What it stores is also in the privacy policy, which is
 * the page the Chrome Web Store links to.
 */
---

<Doc
  title="Chrome extension"
  description="Review changes, commit and open terminals in your workspaces from Chrome's side panel."
  canonicalPath="/docs/extension/"
>
  <p>
    Exeora for Chrome puts the dashboard's Workspace screen in Chrome's side panel, next to whatever
    tab you are on: the pull request you are reviewing, the preview you are testing, the issue you
    are writing. It is the same screen, built from the same code, so what you can do in it is what{" "}
    <a href="/docs/workspace/">Source Control and terminal</a> describes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>

  <p>
    Add <a href={CHROME_EXTENSION_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Exeora</a> from the Chrome Web
    Store, then pin it to the toolbar. Clicking its icon opens the side panel in that window; the
    panel stays open as you switch tabs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sign-in">Signing in</h2>

  <p>
    The panel asks you to sign in with Exeora. Chrome opens a window of its own on the Exeora
    sign-in page, the same one the dashboard uses, and the first time on an account it also asks you
    to authorize the extension. There is no password or key to paste: it is an OAuth sign-in with
    PKCE, and the panel keeps the session it is given.
  </p>

  <p>
    A restart does not sign you out. <strong>Sign out</strong> in the panel's header forgets the
    session in the browser and revokes it on Exeora.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-does">What the panel does</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Pick a project and one of its <a href="/docs/projects/">workspaces</a>, on any of your
      machines or on <a href="/docs/cloud/">Exeora Cloud</a>.
    </li>
    <li>See what changed, read the diffs, stage, commit, pull and push.</li>
    <li>Open terminals on the workspace, which keep running while you move between tabs.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dashboard</strong> in the header opens the workspace you are looking at in the full
      dashboard, and anything the panel does not show (adding a project, a project's settings,
      clients, activity) opens there too.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Everything the panel does goes through the same checks as the dashboard. A project's{" "}
    <a href="/docs/policy/">policy</a> applies to its terminals and Source Control exactly as it
    does there, and the same actions appear in the activity log.
  </p>

  <h2 id="permissions">What it can reach</h2>

  <p>
    The extension asks Chrome for three permissions: the side panel, the identity window it signs in
    through, and its own storage. Its only host permission is <code>exeora.dev</code>. It reads
    nothing from the pages you visit and sends requests nowhere else.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not an MCP client and does not give an AI anything: your agents connect through a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/clients/">client URL</a> as before. The panel is for you, to see and act on what
    they did.
  </p>

  <h2 id="revoke">Revoking it</h2>

  <p>
    In the dashboard, <strong>Settings</strong> shows <strong>Exeora for Chrome</strong> once you
    have approved it: when, and how many browsers are signed in. Revoking it there signs out every
    side panel at once and forgets the approval, so the next sign-in asks for consent again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="self-hosted">On a self-hosted gateway</h2>

  <p>
    The extension signs in against one gateway, fixed when it is built, and a gateway only accepts
    the extension ids it lists. The one in the Chrome Web Store talks to <code>exeora.dev</code>.
    To use it with your own gateway, build it for that gateway and allow its id: see{" "}
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/#chrome-extension">Self-hosting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">What is stored</h2>

  <p>
    In your browser, a refresh token in Chrome's extension storage and a short-lived access token
    in memory. On Exeora, that you approved the extension and one session per signed-in browser.
    The <a href="/privacy/#exeora-for-chrome">privacy policy</a> has the details.
  </p>
</Doc>
